WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.968 position:50% size:92% align:center
The starter stores sixteen FP32 inputs: eight

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:07.411 position:50% size:92% align:center
copies of one point zero zero zero three, then

00:00:07.411 --> 00:00:09.581 position:50% size:92% align:center
eight copies of negative one.

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:13.303 position:50% size:92% align:center
Its question is what happens when each loaded

00:00:13.303 --> 00:00:15.559 position:50% size:92% align:center
value is rounded through FP16

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:17.737 position:50% size:92% align:center
before an FP32 accumulation.

00:00:19.757 --> 00:00:22.343 position:50% size:92% align:center
The device input allocation still uses four

00:00:22.343 --> 00:00:25.170 position:50% size:92% align:center
bytes per element. The conversion happens after

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.056 position:50% size:92% align:center
loading a value into the thread’s working state.

00:00:28.276 --> 00:00:30.985 position:50% size:92% align:center
This particular kernel changes the value

00:00:30.985 --> 00:00:34.100 position:50% size:92% align:center
representation during computation; it does not

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.809 position:50% size:92% align:center
change the input array’s allocated type.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:42.626 position:50% size:92% align:center
Rounding sends one point zero zero zero three to

00:00:42.626 --> 00:00:45.869 position:50% size:92% align:center
exactly one point zero in this FP16 case.

00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:49.636 position:50% size:92% align:center
Negative one is exactly representable and stays

00:00:49.636 --> 00:00:52.730 position:50% size:92% align:center
unchanged. Conversion back to FP32 cannot

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:55.220 position:50% size:92% align:center
recover the discarded difference.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.782 position:50% size:92% align:center
The rounded positive and negative contributions

00:01:00.782 --> 00:01:04.173 position:50% size:92% align:center
cancel to zero. Keeping FP32 operands gives a

00:01:04.173 --> 00:01:06.584 position:50% size:92% align:center
small positive reference result.

00:01:06.804 --> 00:01:10.035 position:50% size:92% align:center
An FP32 accumulator limits further accumulation

00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:12.922 position:50% size:92% align:center
rounding, but it cannot repair information

00:01:12.922 --> 00:01:14.847 position:50% size:92% align:center
removed before the addition.

00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:20.491 position:50% size:92% align:center
Consider a new row alternating one point zero

00:01:20.491 --> 00:01:24.115 position:50% size:92% align:center
and negative zero point five. Both values are

00:01:24.115 --> 00:01:26.531 position:50% size:92% align:center
exactly representable in FP16.

00:01:26.751 --> 00:01:29.432 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict whether the round trip must

00:01:29.432 --> 00:01:31.636 position:50% size:92% align:center
change the operands or the final sum.

00:01:37.456 --> 00:01:40.250 position:50% size:92% align:center
No input value changes in that case, and the

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.789 position:50% size:92% align:center
illustrated sum remains four point zero.

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:46.102 position:50% size:92% align:center
Reason separately about stored types, operand

00:01:46.102 --> 00:01:49.195 position:50% size:92% align:center
conversion and accumulation. A smaller format

00:01:49.195 --> 00:01:51.325 position:50% size:92% align:center
does not by itself establish an

00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:53.249 position:50% size:92% align:center
accuracy loss or a speed-up.
